### Next.js Auth.js Configuration

Source: https://authjs.dev/getting-started/installation?framework=Qwik

Create an auth.ts file for Next.js configuration and set up the API route handler.

```typescript
import NextAuth from "next-auth"

export const { handlers, signIn, signOut, auth } = NextAuth({
  providers: [],
})
```

```typescript
import { handlers } from "@/auth" // Referring to the auth.ts we just created
export const { GET, POST } = handlers
```

--------------------------------

### Next.js Credentials Provider Configuration

Source: https://authjs.dev/getting-started/providers/credentials

Configure the Credentials provider for Next.js applications. This snippet shows how to define credentials and an authorize function to validate user input.

```typescript
import NextAuth from "next-auth"
import Credentials from "next-auth/providers/credentials"

export const {signIn, signOut, auth } = NextAuth({
  providers: [
    Credentials({
      credentials: {
        username: { label: "Username" },
        password: { label: "Password", type: "password" },
      },
      async authorize({ request }) {
        const response = await fetch(request)
        if (!response.ok) return null
        return (await response.json()) ?? null
      },
    }),
  ],
})
```

--------------------------------

### Add User ID to JWT Session

Source: https://authjs.dev/guides/extending-the-session

Extend the JWT session to include the user's ID by modifying the `jwt` and `session` callbacks. This makes the ID available via `token.id` and `session.user.id`.

```typescript
callbacks: {
    jwt({ token, user }) {
      if (user) { // User is available during sign-in
        token.id = user.id
      }
      return token
    },
    session({ session, token }) {
      session.user.id = token.id
      return session
    },
  },
}
```

--------------------------------

### Protecting a Next.js App Router Page

Source: https://authjs.dev/getting-started/session-management/protecting?framework=express

Use the `auth` function in the App Router to retrieve the session and conditionally render content or an unauthenticated message.

```typescript
import { auth } from "@/auth"

export default async function Page() {
  const session = await auth()
  if (!session) return <div>Not authenticated</div>

  return (
    <div>
      <pre>{JSON.stringify(session, null, 2)}</pre>
    </div>
  )
}
```

--------------------------------

### Add Signin Button in Next.js (Server Action)

Source: https://authjs.dev/getting-started/authentication/email

Implement a sign-in button in your Next.js application using a server action. This form submits the user's email to the Postmark provider.

```typescript
import { signIn } from "../../auth.ts"

export function SignIn() {
  return (
    <form
      action={async (formData) => {
        "use server"
        await signIn("postmark", formData)
      }}
    >
      <input type="text" name="email" placeholder="Email" />
      <button type="submit">Signin with Postmark</button>
    </form>
  )
}

```

